({x}^{2}+1)}{{x}^{2}+1}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{({x}^{2}+1)}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemembering that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwe can now calculate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x-4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-114184/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e6b5d85-0b53-580f-9520-6c33463231d5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114184"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-386"] :question/problem "Calculate the indefinite integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\\textrm{d}x = \\arcsin (x) + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-387"] :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2}{1+x^2}\\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+x^2}\\textrm{d}x = \\arctan{x} + C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3890"] :question/problem "If $f'(x) = \\dfrac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{x^2 - 1}} - 2x,$ then $f(x)=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{x^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm dx = \\textrm{arcsec} \\, x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}-2x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\text{arcsec}\\,(x)-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =5\\text{arcsec}\\,(x)-{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-388"] :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {x^2+2}{x^2 + 1}\\,  \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral doesn't look like one of the basic ones. However, we can manipulate the numerator a bit  to make it look more like the denominator, which reveals a more familiar expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1+1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemembering that\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2+1} \\textrm dx = \\arctan(x),\n$$\n\nwe can now calculate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+2}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
